        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: எதிர்",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "எதிர் — etir — \"to oppose; to resist\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "பல மாணவர்கள் இந்தத் திட்டத்தை எதிர்த்தார்கள். pala māṇavarkaḷ intat tiṭṭattai etirttārkaḷ. Many students opposed this plan."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past (I …): எதிர்த்தேன், etirttēṉ."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is opposed takes -ஐ: திட்டத்தை எதிர் (etir). The same word means opposite, as in எதிரே."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "எதிர் (etir) is native Dravidian (DEDR 795): the noun means \"that which is in front, opposite, adverse\", and the verb \"to oppose, to face\". Tamil already uses it in எதிரே, \"opposite\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for giving reasons and opinions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
